---
import ProjectLayout from "../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="distributed-inventory-manager"
  label="Coursework"
  description="An inventory system refactored into a clean MVC structure with automated testing and CI."
  intro="An inventory management system from a group project, which I refactored into a clean Model-View-Controller structure in Python and backed with automated tests and a CI pipeline."
  stack={["Python", "Pytest", "Jenkins", "SonarQube"]}
  links={[
    {
      label: "Code",
      href: "https://github.com/cgallagher-dev/distributed-inventory-manager-project",
    },
    {
      label: "Demo video",
      href: "https://1drv.ms/v/c/c0802369369d875b/Efy4yM0Qf8RGsWp6xpzGFFcBBTwSpYCdB7nFoF5f5F8sng?e=1fHTn6",
    },
  ]}
>
  <section>
    <h2>Structure</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Model</strong> holds the data and business rules, including
        validation, and knows nothing about the interface.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>View</strong> takes input and displays results, so the
        interface can change without touching the logic.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Controller</strong> sits between the two, routing input to the
        right logic and choosing what to show next.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Testing and CI</h2>
    <p>
      Pytest covers the model and controller with unit and integration tests.
      A Jenkins pipeline runs the tests on every change, and SonarQube checks
      the code for bugs and smells before it is merged.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What I took from it</h2>
    <p>
      Splitting the logic out of the interface made the code much easier to
      test, and having the pipeline in place meant I could refactor without
      worrying about breaking things quietly.
    </p>
  </section>
</ProjectLayout>
